---
id: basic-concepts
title: Basic Concepts and Terminology
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This page introduces the basic concepts and terminology used in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-form"},{"type":"text","value":" library. Familiarizing yourself with these concepts will help you better understand and work with the library."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"form-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Form Options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can create options for your form so that it can be shared between multiple forms by using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" function."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"form-instance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Form Instance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Form Instance is an object that represents an individual form and provides methods and properties for working with the form. You create a form instance using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":" function. The function accepts an object with an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onSubmit"},{"type":"text","value":" function, which is called when the form is submitted."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const form = useForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n})","lang":"js"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may also create a form instance without using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" by using the standalone "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":" API:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const form = useForm({\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Field represents a single form input element, such as a text input or a checkbox. Fields are created using the form.Field component provided by the form instance. The component accepts a name prop, which should match a key in the form's default values. It also accepts a scoped slot defined by the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v-slot"},{"type":"text","value":" directive which takes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" object as its argument."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field name=\"fullName\">\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"field-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each field has its own state, which includes its current value, validation status, error messages, and other metadata. You can access a field's state using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field().state"},{"type":"text","value":" property."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const {\n  value,\n  meta: { errors, isValidating },\n} = field.state","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are four states in the metadata that can be useful to see how the user interacts with a field:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"isTouched\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", after the user changes the field or blurs the field"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"isDirty\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", after the field's value has been changed, even if it's been reverted to the default. Opposite of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPristine"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"isPristine\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", until the user changes the field value. Opposite of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isDirty"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"isBlurred\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", after the field has been blurred"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { isTouched, isDirty, isPristine, isBlurred } = field.state.meta","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"image","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states.png","alt":"Field states"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"understanding-isdirty-in-different-libraries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Understanding 'isDirty' in Different Libraries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-Persistent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dirty"},{"type":"text","value":" state"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Libraries"}]},{"type":"text","value":": React Hook Form (RHF), Formik, Final Form."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]},{"type":"text","value":": A field is 'dirty' if its value differs from the default. Reverting to the default value makes it 'clean' again."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistent "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dirty"},{"type":"text","value":" state"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Libraries"}]},{"type":"text","value":": Angular Form, Vue FormKit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior"}]},{"type":"text","value":": A field remains 'dirty' once changed, even if reverted to the default value."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We have chosen the persistent 'dirty' state model. To also support a non-persistent 'dirty' state, we introduce an additional flag:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"isDefaultValue\""}]},{"type":"text","value":", whether the field's current value is the default value"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { isDefaultValue, isTouched } = field.state.meta\n\n// The following line will re-create the non-Persistent `dirty` functionality.\nconst nonPersistentIsDirty = !isDefaultValue","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"image","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states-extended.png","alt":"Field states extended"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"field-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Field API"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Field API is an object provided by a scoped slot using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v-slot"},{"type":"text","value":" directive. This slot receives an argument named "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" that provides methods and properties for working with the field's state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n  <input\n    :name=\"field.name\"\n    :value=\"field.state.value\"\n    @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n    @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n  />\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"validation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-form"},{"type":"text","value":" provides both synchronous and asynchronous validation out of the box. Validation functions can be passed to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Field"},{"type":"text","value":" component using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"validators"},{"type":"text","value":" prop."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<template>\n    <!-- ... -->\n    <form.Field\n        name=\"firstName\"\n        :validators=\"{\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n                !value\n                    ? `A first name is required`\n                    : value.length < 3\n                        ? `First name must be at least 3 characters`\n                        : undefined,\n            onChangeAsync: async ({ value }) => {\n                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n                return value.includes('error') && 'No \"error\" allowed in first name'\n        },\n    }\"\n    >\n        <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n            <input\n                :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n            />\n            <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n        </template>\n    </form.Field>\n    <!-- ... -->\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"validation-with-standard-schema-libraries","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Validation with Standard Schema Libraries"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In addition to hand-rolled validation options, we also support the "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/standard-schema/standard-schema","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Standard Schema"}]},{"type":"text","value":" specification."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can define a schema using any of the libraries implementing the specification and pass it to a form or field validator."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supported libraries include:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://zod.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Zod"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (v3.24.0 or higher)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://valibot.dev/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Valibot"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (v1.0.0 or higher)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://arktype.io/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ArkType"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (v2.1.20 or higher)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/jquense/yup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Yup"}]},{"type":"text","value":" (v1.7.0 or higher)"}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/vue-form'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst form = useForm({\n  // ...\n})\n\nconst onChangeFirstName = z.string().refine(\n  async (value) => {\n    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n    return !value.includes('error')\n  },\n  {\n    message: \"No 'error' allowed in first name\",\n  },\n)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"firstName\"\n    :validators=\"{\n      onChange: z.string().min(3, 'First name must be at least 3 characters'),\n      onChangeAsyncDebounceMs: 500,\n      onChangeAsync: onChangeFirstName,\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field, state }\">\n      <label :htmlFor=\"field.name\">First Name:</label>\n      <input\n        :id=\"field.name\"\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n      />\n      <FieldInfo :state=\"state\" />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactivity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactivity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-form"},{"type":"text","value":" offers various ways to subscribe to form and field state changes, most notably the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" method and the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" component. These methods allow you to optimize your form's rendering performance by only updating components when necessary."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script setup lang=\"ts\">\n// ...\nconst firstName = form.useSelector((state) => state.values.firstName)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Subscribe>\n    <template v-slot=\"{ canSubmit, isSubmitting }\">\n      <button type=\"submit\" :disabled=\"!canSubmit\">\n        {{ isSubmitting ? '...' : 'Submit' }}\n      </button>\n    </template>\n  </form.Subscribe>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"listeners","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Listeners"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-form"},{"type":"text","value":" allows you to react to specific triggers and \"listen\" to them to dispatch side effects."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<template>\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"country\"\n    :listeners=\"{\n      onChange: ({ value }) => {\n        console.log(`Country changed to: ${value}, resetting province`)\n        form.setFieldValue('province', '')\n      },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"More information can be found at "},{"type":"link","href":"./listeners.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Listeners"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: The usage of the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.useField"},{"type":"text","value":" method to achieve reactivity is discouraged since it is designed to be used thoughtfully within the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Field"},{"type":"text","value":" component. You might want to use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.useSelector"},{"type":"text","value":" instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"array-fields","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array Fields"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Array fields allow you to manage a list of values within a form, such as a list of hobbies. You can create an array field using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Field"},{"type":"text","value":" component with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mode=\"array\""},{"type":"text","value":" prop."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When working with array fields, you can use the fields "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pushValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"removeValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"swapValues"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"moveValue"},{"type":"text","value":" methods to add, remove, swap, and move a value from one index to another within the array, respectively. Additional helper methods such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"insertValue"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"replaceValue"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"clearValues"},{"type":"text","value":" are also available for inserting, replacing, and clearing array values."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<template>\n  <form @submit.prevent.stop=\"form.handleSubmit\">\n    <form.Field name=\"hobbies\" mode=\"array\">\n      <template v-slot=\"{ field: hobbiesField }\">\n        <div>\n          Hobbies\n          <div>\n            <div\n              v-if=\"\n                Array.isArray(hobbiesField.state.value) &&\n                !hobbiesField.state.value.length\n              \"\n            >\n              No hobbies found.\n            </div>\n            <div v-else>\n              <div v-for=\"(_, i) in hobbiesField.state.value\" :key=\"i\">\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].name`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Name:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <button\n                        type=\"button\"\n                        @click=\"hobbiesField.removeValue(i)\"\n                      >\n                        X\n                      </button>\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].description`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Description:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <button\n              type=\"button\"\n              @click=\"\n                hobbiesField.pushValue({\n                  name: '',\n                  description: '',\n                  yearsOfExperience: 0,\n                })\n              \"\n            >\n              Add hobby\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </template>\n    </form.Field>\n  </form>\n</template>","lang":"vue"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These are the basic concepts and terminology used in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/vue-form"},{"type":"text","value":" library. Understanding these concepts will help you work more effectively with the library and create complex forms with ease."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"form-options","text":"Form Options","level":2},{"id":"form-instance","text":"Form Instance","level":2},{"id":"field","text":"Field","level":2},{"id":"field-state","text":"Field State","level":2},{"id":"understanding-isdirty-in-different-libraries","text":"Understanding 'isDirty' in Different Libraries","level":2},{"id":"field-api","text":"Field API","level":2},{"id":"validation","text":"Validation","level":2},{"id":"validation-with-standard-schema-libraries","text":"Validation with Standard Schema Libraries","level":2},{"id":"reactivity","text":"Reactivity","level":2},{"id":"listeners","text":"Listeners","level":2},{"id":"array-fields","text":"Array Fields","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThis page introduces the basic concepts and terminology used in the `@tanstack/vue-form` library. Familiarizing yourself with these concepts will help you better understand and work with the library.\n\n## Form Options\n\nYou can create options for your form so that it can be shared between multiple forms by using the `formOptions` function.\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nconst formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})\n```\n\n## Form Instance\n\nA Form Instance is an object that represents an individual form and provides methods and properties for working with the form. You create a form instance using the `useForm` function. The function accepts an object with an `onSubmit` function, which is called when the form is submitted.\n\n```js\nconst form = useForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nYou may also create a form instance without using `formOptions` by using the standalone `useForm` API:\n\n```ts\nconst form = useForm({\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})\n```\n\n## Field\n\nA Field represents a single form input element, such as a text input or a checkbox. Fields are created using the form.Field component provided by the form instance. The component accepts a name prop, which should match a key in the form's default values. It also accepts a scoped slot defined by the `v-slot` directive which takes a `field` object as its argument.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field name=\"fullName\">\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Field State\n\nEach field has its own state, which includes its current value, validation status, error messages, and other metadata. You can access a field's state using the `field().state` property.\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nconst {\n  value,\n  meta: { errors, isValidating },\n} = field.state\n```\n\nThere are four states in the metadata that can be useful to see how the user interacts with a field:\n\n- _\"isTouched\"_, after the user changes the field or blurs the field\n- _\"isDirty\"_, after the field's value has been changed, even if it's been reverted to the default. Opposite of `isPristine`\n- _\"isPristine\"_, until the user changes the field value. Opposite of `isDirty`\n- _\"isBlurred\"_, after the field has been blurred\n\n```ts\nconst { isTouched, isDirty, isPristine, isBlurred } = field.state.meta\n```\n\n![Field states](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states.png)\n\n## Understanding 'isDirty' in Different Libraries\n\nNon-Persistent `dirty` state\n\n- **Libraries**: React Hook Form (RHF), Formik, Final Form.\n- **Behavior**: A field is 'dirty' if its value differs from the default. Reverting to the default value makes it 'clean' again.\n\nPersistent `dirty` state\n\n- **Libraries**: Angular Form, Vue FormKit.\n- **Behavior**: A field remains 'dirty' once changed, even if reverted to the default value.\n\nWe have chosen the persistent 'dirty' state model. To also support a non-persistent 'dirty' state, we introduce an additional flag:\n\n- _\"isDefaultValue\"_, whether the field's current value is the default value\n\n```ts\nconst { isDefaultValue, isTouched } = field.state.meta\n\n// The following line will re-create the non-Persistent `dirty` functionality.\nconst nonPersistentIsDirty = !isDefaultValue\n```\n\n![Field states extended](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states-extended.png)\n\n## Field API\n\nThe Field API is an object provided by a scoped slot using the `v-slot` directive. This slot receives an argument named `field` that provides methods and properties for working with the field's state.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n  <input\n    :name=\"field.name\"\n    :value=\"field.state.value\"\n    @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n    @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n  />\n</template>\n```\n\n## Validation\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` provides both synchronous and asynchronous validation out of the box. Validation functions can be passed to the `form.Field` component using the `validators` prop.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n    <!-- ... -->\n    <form.Field\n        name=\"firstName\"\n        :validators=\"{\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n                !value\n                    ? `A first name is required`\n                    : value.length < 3\n                        ? `First name must be at least 3 characters`\n                        : undefined,\n            onChangeAsync: async ({ value }) => {\n                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n                return value.includes('error') && 'No \"error\" allowed in first name'\n        },\n    }\"\n    >\n        <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n            <input\n                :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n            />\n            <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n        </template>\n    </form.Field>\n    <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Validation with Standard Schema Libraries\n\nIn addition to hand-rolled validation options, we also support the [Standard Schema](https://github.com/standard-schema/standard-schema) specification.\n\nYou can define a schema using any of the libraries implementing the specification and pass it to a form or field validator.\n\nSupported libraries include:\n\n- [Zod](https://zod.dev/) (v3.24.0 or higher)\n- [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/) (v1.0.0 or higher)\n- [ArkType](https://arktype.io/) (v2.1.20 or higher)\n- [Yup](https://github.com/jquense/yup) (v1.7.0 or higher)\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/vue-form'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst form = useForm({\n  // ...\n})\n\nconst onChangeFirstName = z.string().refine(\n  async (value) => {\n    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n    return !value.includes('error')\n  },\n  {\n    message: \"No 'error' allowed in first name\",\n  },\n)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"firstName\"\n    :validators=\"{\n      onChange: z.string().min(3, 'First name must be at least 3 characters'),\n      onChangeAsyncDebounceMs: 500,\n      onChangeAsync: onChangeFirstName,\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field, state }\">\n      <label :htmlFor=\"field.name\">First Name:</label>\n      <input\n        :id=\"field.name\"\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n      />\n      <FieldInfo :state=\"state\" />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Reactivity\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` offers various ways to subscribe to form and field state changes, most notably the `form.useSelector` method and the `form.Subscribe` component. These methods allow you to optimize your form's rendering performance by only updating components when necessary.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\n// ...\nconst firstName = form.useSelector((state) => state.values.firstName)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Subscribe>\n    <template v-slot=\"{ canSubmit, isSubmitting }\">\n      <button type=\"submit\" :disabled=\"!canSubmit\">\n        {{ isSubmitting ? '...' : 'Submit' }}\n      </button>\n    </template>\n  </form.Subscribe>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Listeners\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` allows you to react to specific triggers and \"listen\" to them to dispatch side effects.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"country\"\n    :listeners=\"{\n      onChange: ({ value }) => {\n        console.log(`Country changed to: ${value}, resetting province`)\n        form.setFieldValue('province', '')\n      },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n</template>\n```\n\nMore information can be found at [Listeners](./listeners.md)\n\nNote: The usage of the `form.useField` method to achieve reactivity is discouraged since it is designed to be used thoughtfully within the `form.Field` component. You might want to use `form.useSelector` instead.\n\n## Array Fields\n\nArray fields allow you to manage a list of values within a form, such as a list of hobbies. You can create an array field using the `form.Field` component with the `mode=\"array\"` prop.\n\nWhen working with array fields, you can use the fields `pushValue`, `removeValue`, `swapValues` and `moveValue` methods to add, remove, swap, and move a value from one index to another within the array, respectively. Additional helper methods such as `insertValue`, `replaceValue`, and `clearValues` are also available for inserting, replacing, and clearing array values.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <form @submit.prevent.stop=\"form.handleSubmit\">\n    <form.Field name=\"hobbies\" mode=\"array\">\n      <template v-slot=\"{ field: hobbiesField }\">\n        <div>\n          Hobbies\n          <div>\n            <div\n              v-if=\"\n                Array.isArray(hobbiesField.state.value) &&\n                !hobbiesField.state.value.length\n              \"\n            >\n              No hobbies found.\n            </div>\n            <div v-else>\n              <div v-for=\"(_, i) in hobbiesField.state.value\" :key=\"i\">\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].name`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Name:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <button\n                        type=\"button\"\n                        @click=\"hobbiesField.removeValue(i)\"\n                      >\n                        X\n                      </button>\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].description`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Description:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <button\n              type=\"button\"\n              @click=\"\n                hobbiesField.pushValue({\n                  name: '',\n                  description: '',\n                  yearsOfExperience: 0,\n                })\n              \"\n            >\n              Add hobby\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </template>\n    </form.Field>\n  </form>\n</template>\n```\n\nThese are the basic concepts and terminology used in the `@tanstack/vue-form` library. Understanding these concepts will help you work more effectively with the library and create complex forms with ease.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: basic-concepts\ntitle: Basic Concepts and Terminology\n---\n\nThis page introduces the basic concepts and terminology used in the `@tanstack/vue-form` library. Familiarizing yourself with these concepts will help you better understand and work with the library.\n\n## Form Options\n\nYou can create options for your form so that it can be shared between multiple forms by using the `formOptions` function.\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nconst formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})\n```\n\n## Form Instance\n\nA Form Instance is an object that represents an individual form and provides methods and properties for working with the form. You create a form instance using the `useForm` function. The function accepts an object with an `onSubmit` function, which is called when the form is submitted.\n\n```js\nconst form = useForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nYou may also create a form instance without using `formOptions` by using the standalone `useForm` API:\n\n```ts\nconst form = useForm({\n  onSubmit: async ({ value }) => {\n    // Do something with form data\n    console.log(value)\n  },\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: '',\n    lastName: '',\n    hobbies: [],\n  } as Person,\n})\n```\n\n## Field\n\nA Field represents a single form input element, such as a text input or a checkbox. Fields are created using the form.Field component provided by the form instance. The component accepts a name prop, which should match a key in the form's default values. It also accepts a scoped slot defined by the `v-slot` directive which takes a `field` object as its argument.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field name=\"fullName\">\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Field State\n\nEach field has its own state, which includes its current value, validation status, error messages, and other metadata. You can access a field's state using the `field().state` property.\n\nExample:\n\n```ts\nconst {\n  value,\n  meta: { errors, isValidating },\n} = field.state\n```\n\nThere are four states in the metadata that can be useful to see how the user interacts with a field:\n\n- _\"isTouched\"_, after the user changes the field or blurs the field\n- _\"isDirty\"_, after the field's value has been changed, even if it's been reverted to the default. Opposite of `isPristine`\n- _\"isPristine\"_, until the user changes the field value. Opposite of `isDirty`\n- _\"isBlurred\"_, after the field has been blurred\n\n```ts\nconst { isTouched, isDirty, isPristine, isBlurred } = field.state.meta\n```\n\n![Field states](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states.png)\n\n## Understanding 'isDirty' in Different Libraries\n\nNon-Persistent `dirty` state\n\n- **Libraries**: React Hook Form (RHF), Formik, Final Form.\n- **Behavior**: A field is 'dirty' if its value differs from the default. Reverting to the default value makes it 'clean' again.\n\nPersistent `dirty` state\n\n- **Libraries**: Angular Form, Vue FormKit.\n- **Behavior**: A field remains 'dirty' once changed, even if reverted to the default value.\n\nWe have chosen the persistent 'dirty' state model. To also support a non-persistent 'dirty' state, we introduce an additional flag:\n\n- _\"isDefaultValue\"_, whether the field's current value is the default value\n\n```ts\nconst { isDefaultValue, isTouched } = field.state.meta\n\n// The following line will re-create the non-Persistent `dirty` functionality.\nconst nonPersistentIsDirty = !isDefaultValue\n```\n\n![Field states extended](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/field-states-extended.png)\n\n## Field API\n\nThe Field API is an object provided by a scoped slot using the `v-slot` directive. This slot receives an argument named `field` that provides methods and properties for working with the field's state.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n  <input\n    :name=\"field.name\"\n    :value=\"field.state.value\"\n    @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n    @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n  />\n</template>\n```\n\n## Validation\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` provides both synchronous and asynchronous validation out of the box. Validation functions can be passed to the `form.Field` component using the `validators` prop.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n    <!-- ... -->\n    <form.Field\n        name=\"firstName\"\n        :validators=\"{\n            onChange: ({ value }) =>\n                !value\n                    ? `A first name is required`\n                    : value.length < 3\n                        ? `First name must be at least 3 characters`\n                        : undefined,\n            onChangeAsync: async ({ value }) => {\n                await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n                return value.includes('error') && 'No \"error\" allowed in first name'\n        },\n    }\"\n    >\n        <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n            <input\n                :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n            />\n            <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n        </template>\n    </form.Field>\n    <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Validation with Standard Schema Libraries\n\nIn addition to hand-rolled validation options, we also support the [Standard Schema](https://github.com/standard-schema/standard-schema) specification.\n\nYou can define a schema using any of the libraries implementing the specification and pass it to a form or field validator.\n\nSupported libraries include:\n\n- [Zod](https://zod.dev/) (v3.24.0 or higher)\n- [Valibot](https://valibot.dev/) (v1.0.0 or higher)\n- [ArkType](https://arktype.io/) (v2.1.20 or higher)\n- [Yup](https://github.com/jquense/yup) (v1.7.0 or higher)\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\nimport { useForm } from '@tanstack/vue-form'\nimport { z } from 'zod'\n\nconst form = useForm({\n  // ...\n})\n\nconst onChangeFirstName = z.string().refine(\n  async (value) => {\n    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 1000))\n    return !value.includes('error')\n  },\n  {\n    message: \"No 'error' allowed in first name\",\n  },\n)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"firstName\"\n    :validators=\"{\n      onChange: z.string().min(3, 'First name must be at least 3 characters'),\n      onChangeAsyncDebounceMs: 500,\n      onChangeAsync: onChangeFirstName,\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field, state }\">\n      <label :htmlFor=\"field.name\">First Name:</label>\n      <input\n        :id=\"field.name\"\n        :name=\"field.name\"\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange((e.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\"\n        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n      />\n      <FieldInfo :state=\"state\" />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Reactivity\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` offers various ways to subscribe to form and field state changes, most notably the `form.useSelector` method and the `form.Subscribe` component. These methods allow you to optimize your form's rendering performance by only updating components when necessary.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<script setup lang=\"ts\">\n// ...\nconst firstName = form.useSelector((state) => state.values.firstName)\n</script>\n\n<template>\n  <!-- ... -->\n  <form.Subscribe>\n    <template v-slot=\"{ canSubmit, isSubmitting }\">\n      <button type=\"submit\" :disabled=\"!canSubmit\">\n        {{ isSubmitting ? '...' : 'Submit' }}\n      </button>\n    </template>\n  </form.Subscribe>\n  <!-- ... -->\n</template>\n```\n\n## Listeners\n\n`@tanstack/vue-form` allows you to react to specific triggers and \"listen\" to them to dispatch side effects.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <form.Field\n    name=\"country\"\n    :listeners=\"{\n      onChange: ({ value }) => {\n        console.log(`Country changed to: ${value}, resetting province`)\n        form.setFieldValue('province', '')\n      },\n    }\"\n  >\n    <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n      <input\n        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n      />\n    </template>\n  </form.Field>\n</template>\n```\n\nMore information can be found at [Listeners](./listeners.md)\n\nNote: The usage of the `form.useField` method to achieve reactivity is discouraged since it is designed to be used thoughtfully within the `form.Field` component. You might want to use `form.useSelector` instead.\n\n## Array Fields\n\nArray fields allow you to manage a list of values within a form, such as a list of hobbies. You can create an array field using the `form.Field` component with the `mode=\"array\"` prop.\n\nWhen working with array fields, you can use the fields `pushValue`, `removeValue`, `swapValues` and `moveValue` methods to add, remove, swap, and move a value from one index to another within the array, respectively. Additional helper methods such as `insertValue`, `replaceValue`, and `clearValues` are also available for inserting, replacing, and clearing array values.\n\nExample:\n\n```vue\n<template>\n  <form @submit.prevent.stop=\"form.handleSubmit\">\n    <form.Field name=\"hobbies\" mode=\"array\">\n      <template v-slot=\"{ field: hobbiesField }\">\n        <div>\n          Hobbies\n          <div>\n            <div\n              v-if=\"\n                Array.isArray(hobbiesField.state.value) &&\n                !hobbiesField.state.value.length\n              \"\n            >\n              No hobbies found.\n            </div>\n            <div v-else>\n              <div v-for=\"(_, i) in hobbiesField.state.value\" :key=\"i\">\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].name`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Name:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <button\n                        type=\"button\"\n                        @click=\"hobbiesField.removeValue(i)\"\n                      >\n                        X\n                      </button>\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n                <form.Field :name=\"`hobbies[${i}].description`\">\n                  <template v-slot=\"{ field }\">\n                    <div>\n                      <label :for=\"field.name\">Description:</label>\n                      <input\n                        :id=\"field.name\"\n                        :name=\"field.name\"\n                        :value=\"field.state.value\"\n                        @blur=\"field.handleBlur\"\n                        @input=\"(e) => field.handleChange(e.target.value)\"\n                      />\n                      <FieldInfo :field=\"field\" />\n                    </div>\n                  </template>\n                </form.Field>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n            <button\n              type=\"button\"\n              @click=\"\n                hobbiesField.pushValue({\n                  name: '',\n                  description: '',\n                  yearsOfExperience: 0,\n                })\n              \"\n            >\n              Add hobby\n            </button>\n          </div>\n        </div>\n      </template>\n    </form.Field>\n  </form>\n</template>\n```\n\nThese are the basic concepts and terminology used in the `@tanstack/vue-form` library. Understanding these concepts will help you work more effectively with the library and create complex forms with ease.\n"}
